1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:08,542
Think of a matrix W.

2
00:00:08,542 --> 00:00:12,598
It turns the unit circle into an ellipse.

3
00:00:13,016 --> 00:00:17,678
Let's examine the singular value decomposition of W.

4
00:00:17,678 --> 00:00:24,477
It says any matrix is a combination of three steps, rotate, stretch the axes,

5
00:00:24,477 --> 00:00:25,448
and rotate.

6
00:00:25,883 --> 00:00:30,027
First, a rotation turns the arms onto the axes.

7
00:00:30,027 --> 00:00:34,599
That is the V transpose matrix from SVD.

8
00:00:35,016 --> 00:00:38,863
Next, sigma stretches each axis by its own amount,

9
00:00:38,863 --> 00:00:42,018
here one point three and zero point five.

10
00:00:42,018 --> 00:00:45,162
The circle becomes an ellipse.

11
00:00:45,583 --> 00:00:48,551
Finally, U rotates it into place.

12
00:00:48,551 --> 00:00:54,861
Since rotations never change lengths, all the stretching takes place in sigma.

13
00:00:56,083 --> 00:01:02,355
The two stretch factors, sigma one and sigma two, are the half-axes of the ellipse.

14
00:01:02,355 --> 00:01:05,499
They are called singular values.

15
00:01:05,916 --> 00:01:11,602
If every singular value were exactly one, only the rotations would remain.

16
00:01:11,602 --> 00:01:15,264
Nothing gets stretched, and W would be orthogonal.

17
00:01:15,264 --> 00:01:20,802
If W is not square, the exact name for this is orthonormal columns.

18
00:01:20,802 --> 00:01:25,526
The picture is the same, so we will keep saying orthogonal.

19
00:01:27,783 --> 00:01:33,466
The singular value decomposition lets us watch the stretching along each natural

20
00:01:33,466 --> 00:01:36,947
direction of the matrix, one direction at a time.

21
00:01:37,383 --> 00:01:39,797
In class we worried about one thing,

22
00:01:39,797 --> 00:01:43,619
that the overall scale of the gradients does not explode.

23
00:01:43,619 --> 00:01:47,915
That is the idea behind Xavier initialization.

24
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:52,774
But that is a single number, a rough average over the whole matrix.

25
00:01:52,774 --> 00:01:56,418
It says little about any one direction.

26
00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:59,664
Xavier does not solve every problem.

27
00:01:59,664 --> 00:02:05,568
The average can be fine while one direction still stretches too far and another gets

28
00:02:05,568 --> 00:02:06,974
squeezed to nothing.

29
00:02:07,416 --> 00:02:10,594
We want every direction to keep its size,

30
00:02:10,594 --> 00:02:15,166
so none gets stretched too far and none gets squeezed away.

31
00:02:15,616 --> 00:02:19,213
That is why Muon asks for an orthogonal update,

32
00:02:19,213 --> 00:02:22,734
one where every singular value is exactly one.

33
00:02:22,734 --> 00:02:26,316
Nothing grows, nothing vanishes.

34
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:33,044
Every direction moves together, and the matrix stays healthy step after step.

35
00:02:34,783 --> 00:02:37,897
We want every singular value to be one.

36
00:02:37,897 --> 00:02:40,769
The SVD hands us exactly that.

37
00:02:40,769 --> 00:02:45,937
Write W as U, sigma, V transpose, keep the two rotations,

38
00:02:45,937 --> 00:02:49,927
and replace every stretch in sigma with one.

39
00:02:49,927 --> 00:02:53,931
What remains is just U times V transpose.

40
00:02:54,031 --> 00:03:00,657
But computing an SVD is a long procedure, and it runs slowly on a GPU.

41
00:03:01,116 --> 00:03:08,630
So we want that same result, U times V transpose, without ever computing the SVD.

42
00:03:08,730 --> 00:03:11,154
What can a GPU do cheaply?

43
00:03:11,154 --> 00:03:13,506
It can multiply matrices.

44
00:03:13,506 --> 00:03:16,896
That is the one thing it is built for.

45
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:19,392
So let's start multiplying.

46
00:03:19,392 --> 00:03:25,932
The only matrix we have is W, so the first thing to try is W times W.

47
00:03:26,032 --> 00:03:30,973
But two matrices can only be multiplied when the number of columns of the first

48
00:03:30,973 --> 00:03:33,538
matches the number of rows of the second.

49
00:03:33,538 --> 00:03:40,352
If W has three rows and two columns, W times W does not fit.

50
00:03:40,452 --> 00:03:43,796
The transpose swaps rows and columns.

51
00:03:43,796 --> 00:03:47,650
So W transpose times W always fits.

52
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:50,632
Let's see what that product gives.

53
00:03:51,083 --> 00:03:57,841
To see it, write W with its SVD, as U, sigma, V transpose.

54
00:03:57,841 --> 00:04:06,165
Transposing a product reverses the order, so W transpose is V, sigma, U transpose.

55
00:04:06,265 --> 00:04:08,437
Now put them side by side.

56
00:04:08,437 --> 00:04:11,429
In the middle, U transpose meets U.

57
00:04:11,429 --> 00:04:15,906
The transpose of a rotation is the same rotation done backwards,

58
00:04:15,906 --> 00:04:19,543
and a rotation followed by its reverse does nothing.

59
00:04:19,543 --> 00:04:22,155
So the pair cancels.

60
00:04:22,255 --> 00:04:26,973
What is left is V, sigma times sigma, V transpose.

61
00:04:26,973 --> 00:04:28,775
That is not what we want.

62
00:04:28,775 --> 00:04:33,591
Our target has U on the left, and here U has disappeared.

63
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:35,602
So bring U back.

64
00:04:35,602 --> 00:04:39,250
Multiply by W one more time, on the left.

65
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:45,242
Now the V transpose at the end of W meets the V at the start of our product,

66
00:04:45,242 --> 00:04:47,292
and that pair cancels too.

67
00:04:47,392 --> 00:04:51,637
What survives is U on the left, V transpose on the right,

68
00:04:51,637 --> 00:04:54,096
and sigma three times in between.

69
00:04:54,096 --> 00:04:58,258
This time both rotations are back where they belong.

70
00:04:58,716 --> 00:05:01,030
And the middle is easy to read.

71
00:05:01,030 --> 00:05:05,870
Sigma is diagonal, which means it only holds the singular values,

72
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:07,582
one for each direction.

73
00:05:07,582 --> 00:05:13,540
Multiplying diagonal matrices just multiplies the matching entries.

74
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:18,776
So sigma three times in a row cubes each singular value on its own.

75
00:05:18,776 --> 00:05:22,976
In our example, one point three becomes about two point two,

76
00:05:22,976 --> 00:05:26,616
and zero point five becomes zero point one two five.

77
00:05:26,616 --> 00:05:29,886
No direction disturbs another.

78
00:05:30,316 --> 00:05:32,514
Look back at the two attempts.

79
00:05:32,514 --> 00:05:35,634
Two copies of W lost a rotation.

80
00:05:35,634 --> 00:05:40,756
Three copies kept both, and only the singular values changed.

81
00:05:40,856 --> 00:05:42,618
And we can keep going.

82
00:05:42,618 --> 00:05:48,444
Attach another W transpose times W, and the same cancelling happens again.

83
00:05:48,444 --> 00:05:54,766
U and V transpose stay where they are, and the middle gains two more sigmas.

84
00:05:55,216 --> 00:05:59,544
So the products with an odd number of copies are the ones we can use.

85
00:05:59,544 --> 00:06:02,094
W itself carries sigma.

86
00:06:02,094 --> 00:06:04,708
Three copies carry sigma cubed.

87
00:06:04,708 --> 00:06:09,318
Five copies carry sigma to the fifth, and so on.

88
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:15,035
Every one of these products has the same U on the left and the same V transpose on

89
00:06:15,035 --> 00:06:15,680
the right.

90
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:20,386
So if we multiply each product by a number and add them up,

91
00:06:20,386 --> 00:06:25,092
U and V transpose factor out, and only the middle is a sum.

92
00:06:25,192 --> 00:06:29,989
In that middle, each singular value becomes a number times sigma,

93
00:06:29,989 --> 00:06:33,164
plus a number times sigma cubed, and so on.

94
00:06:33,164 --> 00:06:36,600
A sum of powers like this is a polynomial.

95
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:38,742
Call it p.

96
00:06:38,842 --> 00:06:44,134
So turning W into U times V transpose now means one thing.

97
00:06:44,134 --> 00:06:48,382
We need a p that sends every sigma to one.

98
00:06:48,482 --> 00:06:50,854
Can p do that in a single step?

99
00:06:50,854 --> 00:06:53,892
Then it would have to give one for every input.

100
00:06:53,892 --> 00:07:00,172
But p is made of powers of sigma, so a tiny sigma always gives a tiny output.

101
00:07:00,172 --> 00:07:02,914
One step is not enough.

102
00:07:03,014 --> 00:07:04,946
So we ask for less.

103
00:07:04,946 --> 00:07:11,992
One step only has to bring sigma closer to one, and then we apply p again and again.

104
00:07:12,092 --> 00:07:13,404
Which p?

105
00:07:13,404 --> 00:07:18,944
Every extra term costs more matrix products, so we want as few as possible.

106
00:07:18,944 --> 00:07:21,461
One term alone, a times sigma,

107
00:07:21,461 --> 00:07:25,992
only rescales every singular value by the same number,

108
00:07:25,992 --> 00:07:28,678
so the ellipse stays an ellipse.

109
00:07:28,778 --> 00:07:34,284
So take two terms, a times sigma plus b times sigma cubed.

110
00:07:34,716 --> 00:07:36,678
What should a and b be?

111
00:07:36,678 --> 00:07:39,836
We want the repeated steps to settle at one.

112
00:07:39,836 --> 00:07:45,808
So a sigma that has reached one must stay there, or the steps would never settle.

113
00:07:45,908 --> 00:07:48,936
Put sigma equals one into p.

114
00:07:48,936 --> 00:07:52,834
One cubed is still one, so p gives a plus b.

115
00:07:52,834 --> 00:07:56,878
For one to stay at one, a plus b must equal one.

116
00:07:56,878 --> 00:07:59,640
Call this our first wish.

117
00:08:00,083 --> 00:08:02,359
Staying at one is not enough.

118
00:08:02,359 --> 00:08:06,377
A sigma that is only close to one has to move closer.

119
00:08:06,377 --> 00:08:13,329
So take a sigma that misses one by a small error e, and put one plus e into p.

120
00:08:13,429 --> 00:08:16,089
We need the cube of one plus e.

121
00:08:16,089 --> 00:08:22,947
Multiplied out, it is one, plus three e, plus terms with e squared and e cubed.

122
00:08:22,947 --> 00:08:28,217
When e is small, those last terms are far smaller still, so we drop them.

123
00:08:28,217 --> 00:08:32,921
For example, one point one cubed is one point three three,

124
00:08:32,921 --> 00:08:35,355
very close to one point three.

125
00:08:35,455 --> 00:08:41,085
So p gives a times one plus e, plus b times one plus three e.

126
00:08:41,085 --> 00:08:48,339
Collect the pieces, and that is a plus b, plus a plus three b times e.

127
00:08:48,439 --> 00:08:53,593
The first piece, a plus b, is one, by our first wish.

128
00:08:53,593 --> 00:08:58,567
So p gives one, plus a plus three b times e.

129
00:08:58,567 --> 00:09:01,088
We went in with an error of e,

130
00:09:01,088 --> 00:09:05,793
and we came out with an error of a plus three b times e.

131
00:09:05,893 --> 00:09:09,849
One step multiplies the error by a plus three b.

132
00:09:09,849 --> 00:09:14,805
We want the error to shrink, and the most it can shrink is all the way to nothing.

133
00:09:14,805 --> 00:09:20,077
So our second wish is that a plus three b equals zero.

134
00:09:20,516 --> 00:09:24,854
Two wishes and two unknowns, which is exactly enough.

135
00:09:24,854 --> 00:09:28,440
The second wish says a equals minus three b.

136
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:33,618
Put that into the first, and minus three b plus b equals one,

137
00:09:33,618 --> 00:09:35,570
so b is minus one half.

138
00:09:35,570 --> 00:09:38,412
Then a is three halves.

139
00:09:38,512 --> 00:09:43,772
So p of sigma is three halves sigma, minus one half sigma cubed.

140
00:09:43,772 --> 00:09:47,828
For the matrix, one step is three halves W,

141
00:09:47,828 --> 00:09:51,884
minus one half W times W transpose times W.

142
00:09:51,884 --> 00:09:56,790
That is the Newton–Schulz step, and you could have invented it yourself.

143
00:09:58,216 --> 00:10:01,114
We built p from what happens close to one.

144
00:10:01,114 --> 00:10:03,864
But a real sigma can start anywhere.

145
00:10:03,864 --> 00:10:08,198
So take any positive sigma and apply p over and over.

146
00:10:08,198 --> 00:10:09,640
Where does it end up?

147
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:14,264
That is the question in part (e) of the worksheet.

148
00:10:16,083 --> 00:10:18,031
So this is where we stand.

149
00:10:18,031 --> 00:10:23,057
We wanted U times V transpose without computing an SVD.

150
00:10:23,057 --> 00:10:29,855
Matrix products alone gave us a polynomial, and two wishes fixed its two numbers.

151
00:10:29,955 --> 00:10:33,157
What we have not seen is the iteration itself.

152
00:10:33,157 --> 00:10:35,859
We only know what p does close to one.

153
00:10:35,859 --> 00:10:41,547
Where a sigma ends up when it starts far from one, we find out next time.

